<h2>Europe's entry point for Indian grapes</h2>
<p>The Netherlands functions less as a final destination and more as India's gateway into the wider EU grape market. Rotterdam's port scale and cold-chain distribution infrastructure make it the practical landing point even when the ultimate buyer is in Germany, France or elsewhere — which is worth keeping in mind when reading Dutch import figures, since they capture more than domestic Dutch demand alone.</p>
<p>India's grape export season is short and intense: roughly January through April, peaking in February-March, timed to fill the gap between the end of Southern Hemisphere supply and the start of Northern Hemisphere harvests.</p>

<h2>Variety and specification</h2>
<p>Thompson Seedless is the export mainstay, with Flame Seedless and (seasonally) Crimson Seedless covering red-grape demand, and Sonaka — a Thompson selection — also widely available. Specify berry size (16-18mm or 18mm+) and bunch weight expectations; mixed sizing within a carton is a common quality complaint on under-specified orders.</p>

<h2>What actually determines arrival quality on this route</h2>
<p>Given the 18-24 day transit to Rotterdam — the longest we quote at real volume for a perishable — three things matter more here than almost anywhere else:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pre-cooling speed.</strong> Field heat removed slowly costs shelf life that can't be recovered later, however well the container is subsequently managed.</li>
  <li><strong>SO2 pad calibration.</strong> Sulphur dioxide generator pads inside the carton suppress botrytis (grey mould) over the long sea transit. Too little and mould develops; too much and bleaching occurs. Packing needs to match the actual transit length.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistent 0-2°C storage.</strong> No margin for temperature excursions on a route this long.</li>
</ul>

<h2>EU residue compliance is not optional in practice</h2>
<p>EU maximum residue limits are strict and actively enforced at the border, and pesticide residue is the single most common cause of Indian fresh produce rejection entering the EU. We strongly recommend agreeing pre-shipment residue testing before the goods ship rather than treating it as something to sort out if a problem arises. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</p>

<h2>TRACES registration</h2>
<p>Phytosanitary certificates for EU-bound plant products are registered through the EU's TRACES platform. This is typically managed on the importer's side, using the certificate documentation we provide — worth confirming the process with your importer before your first shipment if this is new territory for either side.</p>

<h2>What the verified data shows</h2>
<p>FY2023-24 figures put total Indian grape exports at 343,982 MT valued at USD 417.07 million. A separate 2025-26 destination ranking names the Netherlands, Russia, the UK, the UAE and Bangladesh as the top five markets in that order, though without individual country values in the source we could verify — we've reported the ranking honestly rather than inventing figures to fill the gap.</p>
